{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"A public no-minimum $50/sample academic 3' RNA-seq service on Illumina sits at about a third of Baylor's internal core price and at 11-20% of the $250-450 per sample cores charged.","detail":"Plasmidsaurus lists $50 academic and $80 industry per sample for ~10M deduplicated 3' reads (~20M raw), with no minimum order or quote process. Baylor's Genomic & RNA Profiling Core (Feb 2026 list, internal) charges $160/sample for batches under 24 and $150 for 24 or more, for ~10M SR75 reads. $50/$150 = 33%, or 31% of $160. GenomeWeb (search summary) quotes a customer whose university core charged $250-450 and took weeks, so $50 is 11-20% of that. Research buyers can therefore get a 3-5x price cut on an Illumina workflow by outsourcing, which fits the 2A claim that research customers fight on price.","url":"https://plasmidsaurus.com/rna","date":"2025-10 launch; prices read 2026-10-12","source_name":"Plasmidsaurus; Baylor GARP price list (cdn.bcm.edu, Feb 2026)","source_type":"price_list","quote":"There is no minimum order size or quote process.","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known list has the F727 $50/sample RNA-seq service as a one-line demand note. This adds the Baylor comparison, the $250-450 core baseline and derived multiples."},
